---
import Article from '../../research/latent-signals-activation-probing.md';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Latent Signals: what model internals tell us before the loss curve does';
const description = 'A practical primer on activation probing during fine-tuning runs, and the early signals that predict whether your model is actually learning what you think it is.';
---

<Layout title={`${pageTitle} · Latentsig AI Research`} description={description}>
<div class="reading-progress" aria-hidden="true"><span id="progress-bar"></span></div>


    <div class="research-page">
      <header class="hero">
        <img src="/images/research/latent-signals/activation-manifold-hero.jpg" alt="Abstract layered activation manifolds revealing a coherent signal direction" width="1672" height="941" loading="eager" />
        <div class="hero-wash"></div>
        <div class="hero-grid"></div>
        <div class="hero-inner">
          <a href="/insights/" class="back-link"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_back</span> Insights</a>
          <div class="eyebrow-row">
            <span class="eyebrow">Featured research</span>
            <span class="read-time">18 min read</span>
          </div>
          <h1>Latent Signals: what model internals tell us <em>before the loss curve does</em></h1>
          <p>{description}</p>
          <div class="author-row">
            <span class="author-mark">λ</span>
            <span><strong>Latentsig AI Research</strong><small>May 2026 · Practitioner synthesis</small></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="signal-strip" aria-label="Research thesis">
        <div><span>01</span><strong>Loss says whether optimization is moving.</strong><small>It does not identify the representation being learned.</small></div>
        <div><span>02</span><strong>Probes expose decodable structure.</strong><small>Transfer and controls distinguish concepts from shortcuts.</small></div>
        <div><span>03</span><strong>Interventions test use.</strong><small>Causality is a higher bar than classification accuracy.</small></div>
      </section>

      <div class="page-grid">
        <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <span>On this page</span>
          <a href="#why-loss-is-necessaryand-structurally-incomplete">Why loss is incomplete</a>
          <a href="#what-an-activation-probe-actually-measures">What a probe measures</a>
          <a href="#a-frontier-model-reality-check">Frontier model access</a>
          <a href="#the-checkpoint-protocol-we-recommend">Checkpoint protocol</a>
          <a href="#reading-the-signals">Reading the signals</a>
          <a href="#key-considerations-and-potential-blind-spots">Blind spots</a>
          <a href="#failure-modes-that-invalidate-a-probe-dashboard">Failure modes</a>
          <a href="#references">References</a>
          <div class="toc-note"><b>Evidence label</b><p>Framework figures are explanatory; illustrative curves and heatmaps are explicitly marked schematic.</p></div>
        </aside>

        <article class="article-body">
          <div class="editor-note">
            <span>Research position</span>
            <p>Activation probing is best treated as observability for model development: an additional instrument alongside loss and behavior, not a mind reader or a replacement for evaluation.</p>
          </div>

          <Article />
        </article>
      </div>

      <section class="closing">
        <span>Latentsig AI Research</span>
        <h2>Instrument the learning process, not just the final answer.</h2>
        <p>We help teams design model diagnostics, evaluation systems, and interpretability experiments for high-stakes fine-tuning runs.</p>
        <a href="/contact">Discuss your model run <span>↗</span></a>
      </section>
    </div>


    <script>
      const updateProgress = () => {
        const article = document.querySelector('.article-body');
        const bar = document.querySelector('#progress-bar');
        if (!article || !bar) return;
        const rect = article.getBoundingClientRect();
        const total = article.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
        const read = Math.min(Math.max(-rect.top, 0), total);
        bar.style.width = `${total > 0 ? (read / total) * 100 : 0}%`;
      };
      updateProgress();
      addEventListener('scroll', updateProgress, { passive: true });
    </script>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  :root{--bg:#08101f;--surface:#0d1729;--surface-2:#121d32;--line:#273149;--ink:#e8edff;--muted:#a6aec5;--violet:#a9abff;--violet-2:#7c80ff;--cyan:#86d2ff;--amber:#ffb783;--serif:"Hanken Grotesk",sans-serif;--body:"Geist",sans-serif;--mono:"DM Mono",monospace}
